We investigate how actuator placement and observation availability affect constrained synchronization in a three-state Hopfield chaotic network, using proximal policy optimization (PPO) to learn a bounded single-input controller. Local auxiliary indicators guide candidate-node screening, and experiments examine synchronization error together with control effort. Node 3 supports low-residual synchronization with reduced policy observations, whereas Node 2 is more sensitive to information loss. A matched-budget, three-seed comparison with soft actor-critic (SAC) shows scenario-dependent performance: PPO has lower nominal Node 2 MAE (0.0930 versus 0.1173), while SAC has lower MAE at the largest tested mismatch (0.1513 versus 0.1703). Fixed-gain feedback, LQR, and sliding-mode control provide traditional references. An all-subset actuator ablation quantifies the error--effort cost of using fewer control channels. State boundedness is established analytically; negative finite-time conditional Lyapunov estimates on evaluated PPO trajectories provide local numerical evidence, not a synchronization stability certificate. Based on the synchronized chaotic trajectories, an image encryption and decryption scheme is constructed using Logistic-map diffusion and dynamic DNA coding. Statistical analysis shows that the encrypted images exhibit reduced statistical redundancy. The control results support a case-specific constrained-design tradeoff rather than universal superiority of PPO or single-input actuation.
